      en: "Artesonraju is one of the most distinctive mountain peaks in the Cordillera Blanca of Peru, reaching an elevation of 6,025 meters above sea level. It is globally recognized for its near-perfect pyramidal shape, which is frequently cited as the inspiration for the iconic Paramount Pictures film studio logo. Located within the Huascarán National Park between the Santa Cruz and Parón valleys, the mountain is a prime target for alpinists seeking technical ice and snow climbing challenges. The first ascent was recorded in August 1932 by an expedition led by Erwin Schneider and Brooke Heckstall-Smith. Due to the extreme steepness of its flanks, often ranging between 45 and 55 degrees, climbing the peak requires advanced ice climbing skills. At the base of the mountain lies the turquoise Laguna Parón, the largest natural lake in the Cordillera Blanca, fed by meltwater from surrounding glaciers. Artesonraju remains a symbol of the pristine beauty of the Andes and a cornerstone of Peru's high-altitude geographical identity.",
